{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs GNU C++11 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following GNU C++11 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nThere is the faculty of Computer Science in Berland. In the social net \"TheContact!\" for each course of this faculty there is the special group whose name equals the year of university entrance of corresponding course of students at the university. Each of students joins the group of his course and joins all groups for which the year of student's university entrance differs by no more than x from the year of university entrance of this student, where x \u2014 some non-negative integer. A value x is not given, but it can be uniquely determined from the available data. Note that students don't join other groups. You are given the list of groups which the student Igor joined. According to this information you need to determine the year of Igor's university entrance.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the GNU C++11 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    cin >> n;\n    vector<int> arr(n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        cin >> arr[i];\n    }\n    \n    sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());\n    \n    int best_value = arr[1];\n    int max_min_dist = min(arr[1] - arr[0], arr[n-1] - arr[1]);\n    \n    for (int i = 2; i < n - 1; ++i) {\n        int min_dist = min(arr[i] - arr[0], arr[n-1] - arr[i]);\n        if (min_dist > max_min_dist) {\n            max_min_dist = min_dist;\n            best_value = arr[i];\n        }\n    }\n    \n    cout << best_value;\n    return 0;\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    cin >> n;\n    vector<int> arr(n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        cin >> arr[i];\n    }\n    \n    sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());\n    \n    // The student joins their own course group and all groups within x years\n    // So the joined groups form a contiguous range [entrance_x - x, entrance_x + x]\n    // The middle of this range is the entrance year\n    // Since the range is contiguous and symmetric around entrance year,\n    // the entrance year should be the median of the sorted list\n    \n    // For a contiguous range of groups, the entrance year is the middle value\n    // If n is odd, it's arr[n/2]\n    // If n is even, it could be either arr[n/2-1] or arr[n/2], but since the range\n    // is symmetric, the entrance year should be the average or one of the middle values\n    \n    // Actually, the key insight: if the student entered in year Y with tolerance x,\n    // they join groups [Y-x, Y-x+1, ..., Y, ..., Y+x]\n    // So the groups form a contiguous arithmetic sequence with difference 1\n    // The entrance year Y is the midpoint of the smallest and largest group years\n    int entrance_year = (arr[0] + arr[n-1]) / 2;\n    \n    cout << entrance_year;\n    return 0;\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.9\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-7.15